- Story & Context: Decades after the Pachuca depths were flooded to drown out lingering coal fires, local salvagers entered the half-submerged catacomb known as La Capilla and found the ventilation grid encrusted with jagged, calcified mineral glass. Striking the crystalline flues with iron crowbars produced an undulating, eternal drone that shook the stagnant green groundwater, causing the drowned mine to murmur with the ghost of a thousand syncopated night shifts. Carved into the salt-rimed zinc casing was the final anonymous inscription of the shift: "The mountain dances when the air goes cold."
